1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:06,118
Chapter 13. Good progress.

2
00:00:08,158 --> 00:00:12,800
Far into the night, while the other creatures slept, Charlotte worked on her

3
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:19,039
web. First, she ripped out a few of the orb lines near the center. She left the

4
00:00:19,039 --> 00:00:24,560
radial lines alone, as they were needed for support. As she worked, her eight

5
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:28,800
legs were a great help to her.

6
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:34,880
So were her teeth. She loved to weave, and she was an expert at it. When she

7
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:41,880
was finished ripping things out, her web looked something like this.

8
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:50,719
A spider can produce several kinds of thread. She uses a dry tough thread for

9
00:00:50,719 --> 00:00:56,238
foundation lines, and she uses a sticky thread for snare lines, the ones that

10
00:00:56,238 --> 00:01:04,079
catch and hold insects. Charlotte decided to use her dry thread for writing the new message.

11
00:01:04,079 --> 00:01:09,760
If I write the word terrific with sticky thread, she thought, every bug that

12
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:15,039
comes along will get stuck in it and spoil the effect. Now, let's see. The

13
00:01:15,039 --> 00:01:20,560
first letter is T. Charlotte climbed to a point at the top of the left hand side

14
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:25,920
of the web. Swinging her spinteretses into position, she attached her thread

15
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,198
and then dropped down.

16
00:01:29,198 --> 00:01:35,118
As she dropped, her spinning tubes went into action and she let out thread. At

17
00:01:35,118 --> 00:01:37,920
the bottom, she attached the thread.

18
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:43,920
This formed the upright part of the letter T. Charlotte was not satisfied,

19
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:51,279
however. She climbed up and made another attachment right next to the first.

20
00:01:51,279 --> 00:01:55,359
Then she carried the line down so that she had a double line instead of a

21
00:01:55,359 --> 00:02:00,399
single line. It'll show up better if I make the whole thing with double lines.

22
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:04,800
She climbed back up, moved over about an inch to the left, touched her

23
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:08,880
spinterettes to the web, and then carried a line across to the right,

24
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:15,279
forming the top of the tea. She repeated this, making it double. Her eight legs

25
00:02:15,279 --> 00:02:19,198
were very busy helping.

26
00:02:19,198 --> 00:02:24,719
Now for the E. Charlotte got so interested in her work, she began to

27
00:02:24,719 --> 00:02:30,079
talk to herself as though to cheer herself on. If you had been sitting

28
00:02:30,079 --> 00:02:34,560
quietly in the barn cellar that evening, you would have heard something like

29
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:40,000
this. Now for the R. Up we go. Attach.

30
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,719
Descend. Pay out line. Wo. Attach. Good.

31
00:02:46,719 --> 00:02:56,479
Up you go. Repeat. Attach. Descend. Pay out line. Wo girl. Steady now. Attach.

32
00:02:56,479 --> 00:03:04,158
Climb. Attach. Over to the right. Pay out line. Attach. Now right and down.

33
00:03:04,158 --> 00:03:10,238
And swing that loop. And around. And around. Now in to the left. Attach.

34
00:03:10,238 --> 00:03:13,760
Climb. Repeat.

35
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:17,198
Okay, easy. Keep those lines together.

36
00:03:17,198 --> 00:03:25,439
Now then, out and down for the leg of the R. Pay out line. Wo! Attach! Ascend!

37
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:31,840
Repeat! Good girl! And so, talking to herself, the spider worked at her

38
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:37,679
difficult task. When it was completed, she felt hungry. She ate a small bug

39
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:42,800
that she had been saving. Then, she slept.

40
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:48,800
Next morning, Wilbur arose and stood beneath the web. He breathed the morning

41
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:55,118
air into his lungs. Drops of dew catching the sun made the web stand out

42
00:03:55,118 --> 00:04:01,920
clearly. When Lurvy arrived with breakfast, there was the handsome pig.

43
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:09,039
And over him, woven neatly in black letters, was the word terrific.

44
00:04:09,039 --> 00:04:12,399
Another miracle.

45
00:04:12,399 --> 00:04:15,439
Lurvy rushed and called Mr. Zuckerman.

46
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:17,679
Mr. Zuckerman rushed and called Mrs.

47
00:04:17,679 --> 00:04:22,800
Zuckerman. Mrs. Zuckerman ran to the phone and called the arables. The

48
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:28,479
arables climbed into their truck and hurried over. Everybody stood at the pig

49
00:04:28,479 --> 00:04:35,599
pin and stared at the web and read the word over and over while Wilbur, who

50
00:04:35,599 --> 00:04:41,599
really felt terrific, stood quietly swelling out his chest and swinging his

51
00:04:41,599 --> 00:04:46,000
snout from side to side.

52
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,279
Terrific, breathed Zuckerman in joyful admiration.

53
00:04:51,279 --> 00:04:55,920
Edith, you better phone the reporter on the Weekly Chronicle and tell him what

54
00:04:55,920 --> 00:05:00,399
has happened. He will want to know about this. He may want to bring a

55
00:05:00,399 --> 00:05:06,000
photographer. There isn't a pig in the whole state that is as terrific as our

56
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,479
pig." The news spread. People who had journeyed to see Wilbur when he was some

57
00:05:12,479 --> 00:05:18,880
pig came back again to see him now that he was terrific.

58
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:23,198
That afternoon, when Mr. Zuckerman went to milk the cows and clean out the

59
00:05:23,198 --> 00:05:30,639
tie-ups. He was still thinking about what a wondrous pig he owned. Larvey, he

60
00:05:30,639 --> 00:05:37,679
called, "There is to be no more cow manure thrown down into that pig pan. I

61
00:05:37,679 --> 00:05:44,719
have a terrific pig. I want that pig to have clean, bright straw every day for

62
00:05:44,719 --> 00:05:48,238
his bedding. Understand?"

63
00:05:48,639 --> 00:05:55,599
Oh yes, sir, said Lurvy. Furthermore, said Mr. Zuckerman, I want you to start

64
00:05:55,599 --> 00:05:59,760
building a crate for Wilbur, I have decided to take the pig to the county

65
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:05,118
fair on September 6th. Make the crate large and paint it green with gold

66
00:06:05,118 --> 00:06:17,520
letters. What will the letter say? asked Lurvy. They should say Zuckerman's famous pig.

67
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:23,359
Lurvy picked up a pitchfork and walked away to get some clean straw. Having

68
00:06:23,359 --> 00:06:29,599
such an important pig was going to mean plenty of extra work. He could see that.

69
00:06:29,599 --> 00:06:34,880
Below the apple orchard at the end of a path was the dump where Mr. Zuckerman

70
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:40,800
threw all sorts of trash and stuff that nobody wanted anymore.

71
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:47,039
Here in a small clearing hidden by young alders and wild raspberry bushes was an

72
00:06:47,039 --> 00:06:54,880
astonishing pile of old bottles and empty tin cans and dirty rags and bits

73
00:06:54,880 --> 00:07:01,520
of metal and broken bottles and broken hinges and broken springs and dead

74
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:10,560
batteries and last month's magazines and old discarded dish mops and tattered overalls.

75
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:16,960
and rusty spikes and leaky pales and forgotten stoppers and useless junk of

76
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:24,399
all kinds, including a wrongsized crank for a broken ice cream freezer.

77
00:07:24,399 --> 00:07:27,439
Templeton knew the dump and liked it.

78
00:07:27,439 --> 00:07:33,118
There were good hiding places there, excellent cover for a rat, and there was

79
00:07:33,118 --> 00:07:39,359
usually a tin can with food still clinging to the inside.

80
00:07:39,359 --> 00:07:44,800
Templeton was down there now, rumaging around. When he returned to the barn, he

81
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:50,078
carried in his mouth an advertisement he had torn from a crumpled magazine.

82
00:07:50,078 --> 00:07:57,520
"How's this?" he asked, showing the ad to Charlotte. "It says crunchy. Crunchy

83
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:02,078
would be a good word to write in your web."

84
00:08:02,078 --> 00:08:07,439
"Just the wrong idea," replied Charlotte. "Couldn't be worse. We don't

85
00:08:07,439 --> 00:08:12,238
want Zuckerman to think Wilbur is crunchy. He might start thinking about

86
00:08:12,238 --> 00:08:19,118
crisp crunchy bacon and tasty ham. That would put ideas into his head. We must

87
00:08:19,118 --> 00:08:24,960
advertise Wilbur's noble qualities, not his tastiness. Go get another word,

88
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:32,158
please, Templeton. The rat looked disgusted, but he sneaked away to the dump and was

89
00:08:32,158 --> 00:08:37,198
back in a while with a strip of cotton cloth.

90
00:08:37,198 --> 00:08:43,439
How's this? He asked. It's a label off an old shite. Charlotte examined the

91
00:08:43,439 --> 00:08:47,760
label. It said pre-shrunk.

92
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:54,158
I'm sorry, Templeton, she said. But pre-shrunk is out of the question. We

93
00:08:54,158 --> 00:09:00,319
want Zuckerman to think Wilbur is nicely filled out, not all shrunk up. I'll have

94
00:09:00,319 --> 00:09:08,078
to ask you to try again. What do you think I am? A messenger boy? grumbled the rat.

95
00:09:08,078 --> 00:09:15,439
I'm not going to spend all my time chasing down to the dump after advertising material.

96
00:09:15,439 --> 00:09:18,960
Just once more, please, said Charlotte.

97
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:23,279
I'll tell you what I'll do, said Templeton. I know where there's a

98
00:09:23,279 --> 00:09:26,238
package of soap flakes in the woodshed.

99
00:09:26,238 --> 00:09:31,039
It has writing on it. I'll bring you a piece of the package.

100
00:09:31,039 --> 00:09:37,920
He climbed the rope that hung on the wall and disappeared through a hole in the ceiling.

101
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:42,800
When he came back, he had a strip of blue and white cardboard in his teeth.

102
00:09:42,800 --> 00:09:54,078
"Eh," he said triumphantly. "How's that?" Charlotte read the words. "With new radiant action."

103
00:09:54,078 --> 00:10:00,479
"What does it mean?" asked Charlotte, who had never used any soap flakes in her life.

104
00:10:00,479 --> 00:10:06,880
How should I know? said Templeton. You asked for words and I brought them. I

105
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:12,520
suppose the next thing you want me to fetch is a dictionary.

106
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:19,198
Together they studied the soad with new radiant action, repeated Charlotte

107
00:10:19,198 --> 00:10:27,279
slowly. Wilbur, she called. Wilbur, who was asleep in the straw, jumped up. Run

108
00:10:27,279 --> 00:10:33,520
around, commanded Charlotte. I want to see you in action to see if you are radiant.

109
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:41,520
Wilbur raced to the end of his yard. Now back again faster, said Charlotte.

110
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:45,359
Wilbur galloped back. His skin shone.

111
00:10:45,359 --> 00:10:48,880
His tail had a fine tight curl in it.

112
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:51,920
"Jump into the air!" cried Charlotte.

113
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:56,880
Wilbur jumped as high as he could. Keep your knees straight and touch the ground

114
00:10:56,880 --> 00:11:02,960
with your ears, called Charlotte. Wilbur obeyed.

115
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:08,158
Do a back flip with a half twist in it, cried Charlotte. Wilbur went over

116
00:11:08,158 --> 00:11:13,198
backwards, writhing and twisting as he went.

117
00:11:13,198 --> 00:11:19,279
Okay, Wilbur, said Charlotte, you can go back to sleep. Okay, Templeton, the

118
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:28,158
soapad will do, I guess. I'm not sure Wilbur's action is exactly radiant, but it's interesting.

119
00:11:28,158 --> 00:11:32,479
Actually, said Wilbur. I feel radiant.

120
00:11:32,479 --> 00:11:37,760
Do you? said Charlotte, looking at him with affection. Well, you're a good

121
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:41,679
little pig, and radiant you shall be.

122
00:11:41,679 --> 00:11:47,920
I'm in this thing pretty deep now. I might as well go the limit.

123
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:54,399
Tired from his romp, Wilbur lay down in the clean straw. He closed his eyes. The

124
00:11:54,399 --> 00:11:59,520
straw seemed scratchy, not as comfortable as the cow manure, which was

125
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:04,719
always delightfully soft to lie in, so he pushed the straw to one side and

126
00:12:04,719 --> 00:12:11,279
stretched out in the manure. Wilbur sighed. It had been a busy day, his

127
00:12:11,279 --> 00:12:15,118
first day of being terrific.

128
00:12:15,118 --> 00:12:20,158
Dozens of people had visited his yard during the afternoon, and he had had to

129
00:12:20,158 --> 00:12:27,359
stand and pose, looking as terrific as he could. Now he was tired. Fern had

130
00:12:27,359 --> 00:12:32,319
arrived and seated herself quietly on her stool in the corner. "Tell me a

131
00:12:32,319 --> 00:12:40,399
story, Charlotte," said Wilbur, as he lay waiting for sleep to come. "Tell me a story."

132
00:12:40,399 --> 00:12:46,800
So Charlotte, although she too was tired, did what Wilbur wanted.

133
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:53,279
Once upon a time, she began, "I had a beautiful cousin who managed to build

134
00:12:53,279 --> 00:13:00,079
her web across a small stream. One day, a tiny fish leaped into the air and got

135
00:13:00,079 --> 00:13:06,718
tangled in the web. My cousin was very much surprised. Of course,

136
00:13:06,718 --> 00:13:14,478
the fish was thrashing wildly. My cousin hardly dared tackle it, but she did. She

137
00:13:14,478 --> 00:13:20,318
swooped down and threw great masses of wrapping material around the fish and

138
00:13:20,318 --> 00:13:27,040
fought bravely to capture it. "Did she succeed?" asked Wilbur. "It was a never

139
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:30,079
to be forgotten battle," said Charlotte.

140
00:13:30,079 --> 00:13:36,079
"There was the fish caught only by one fin, and its tail wildly thrashing and

141
00:13:36,079 --> 00:13:39,200
shining in the sun.

142
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:44,558
There was the web sagging dangerously under the weight of the fish. "How much

143
00:13:44,558 --> 00:13:50,638
did the fish weigh?" asked Wilbur eagerly. "I don't know," said Charlotte.

144
00:13:50,638 --> 00:13:56,478
"There was my cousin slipping in, dodging out, beaten mercilessly over the

145
00:13:56,478 --> 00:13:59,120
head by the wildly thrashing fish.

146
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:04,959
Dancing in, dancing out, throwing her threads, and fighting hard. First she

147
00:14:04,959 --> 00:14:11,120
threw a left around the tail. The fish lashed back. Then a left to the tail and

148
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,478
a right to the midsection.

149
00:14:14,478 --> 00:14:20,398
The fish lashed back. Then she dodged to one side and threw a right and another

150
00:14:20,398 --> 00:14:26,318
right to the fin. Then a hard left to the head while the web swayed and

151
00:14:26,318 --> 00:14:33,040
stretched. Then what happened? asked Wilbur. Nothing, said Charlotte. The

152
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:41,760
fish lost the fight. My cousin wrapped it up so tight it couldn't budge.

153
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:45,120
Then what happened? asked Wilbur.

154
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:50,799
Nothing, said Charlotte. My cousin kept the fish for a while and then when she

155
00:14:50,799 --> 00:14:58,318
got good and ready, she ate it. Tell me another story, begged Wilbur. So

156
00:14:58,318 --> 00:15:04,519
Charlotte told him about another cousin of hers who was an aeronaut.

157
00:15:04,638 --> 00:15:12,239
What is an aeronaut?" asked Wilbur. "A balloonist," said Charlotte. "My cousin

158
00:15:12,239 --> 00:15:17,679
used to stand on her head and let out enough thread to form a balloon. Then

159
00:15:17,679 --> 00:15:23,760
she'd let go and be lifted into the air and carried upward on the warm wind."

160
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:30,478
"Is that true?" asked Wilbur. "Or are you just making it up?" "It's true,"

161
00:15:30,478 --> 00:15:37,040
replied Charlotte. I have some very remarkable cousins.

162
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:41,200
And now, Wilbur, it's time you went to sleep.

163
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:48,718
Sing something, begged Wilbur, closing his eyes. So Charlotte sang a lullabi

164
00:15:48,718 --> 00:15:58,079
while crickets chirped in the grass and the barn grew dark. This was the song she sang.

165
00:15:58,079 --> 00:16:05,519
Sleep, sleep, my love, my only deep, deep in the dung and the dark. Be not

166
00:16:05,519 --> 00:16:12,719
afraid and be not lonely. This is the hour when frogs and thrushes praise the

167
00:16:12,719 --> 00:16:15,520
world from the woods and the rushes.

168
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:22,959
Rest from care, my one and only, deep in the dung and the dark. But Wilbur was

169
00:16:22,959 --> 00:16:31,559
already asleep. When the song ended, Fern got up and went home.

170
00:16:32,478 --> 00:16:33,000
Chapter 14.
